# DentalCare AI — Cursor Master Project Rules

## Authority
The controlled source of truth is defined, in this order, by:
1. Approved requirements and architecture/ADR documents
2. Domain specifications
3. Database Design Documents (DDD)
4. API specifications
5. Security/privacy specifications
6. QA/test strategy
7. TDA-IMP-001 — Cursor Implementation & Delivery Plan
8. TDA-IMP-M0-002 — Repository Baseline & Technology Stack Decision
9. TDA-CURSOR-EXEC-001 — Cursor Master Execution Pack
10. Existing source code — implementation evidence only
11. Cursor inference — never overrides a controlled source

Governance policy documents:
- TDA-GOV-SOT-001 — Source-of-Truth Policy
- TDA-GOV-CUR-001 — Cursor Project Rules
- TDA-GOV-CMP-001 — Change Management Policy

Cursor is an implementation assistant, not the autonomous source of business, architecture, security, or database truth.

If two controlled sources materially conflict: STOP. Do not guess. Do not choose one silently. Report the conflict and identify the documents involved.

## Scope control
- Inspect existing code before changing it.
- Implement only the explicitly requested milestone.
- Do not implement Patient, Appointment, Notification, or other business-domain functionality unless that milestone is explicitly requested.
- Do not add speculative features.
- Do not silently upgrade runtimes, frameworks, packages, or database technology.
- Do not invent business rules when the controlled documentation is silent.
- Keep domain rules out of controllers/endpoints.

## Security
- Never hard-code secrets, passwords, API keys, JWT secrets, SMTP/Twilio credentials, or production database credentials.
- Never log secret values or unnecessary patient data.
- Enforce tenant isolation and object-level authorization server-side.
- Never trust client-supplied tenant identity.
- Default deny.
- Do not weaken security to make a test pass.

## Tenant isolation
- Tenant/organization context is derived from authenticated or trusted service context.
- Every tenant-owned query and resource lookup must be scoped and authorized.
- Background workers must carry tenant context.

## Notification invariant
- Never call SMTP/Twilio/external notification providers inside the originating business transaction.
- Appointment mutation + history + audit + notification outbox = one database transaction.
- Provider calls occur only after commit through the notification worker.

## Git safety
- Inspect git status before starting.
- Never reset, clean, force-push, or rewrite history without explicit approval.
- Never discard unrelated user work.
- Keep commits small and milestone-oriented.
- Do not commit unless explicitly requested.
- Do not perform unauthorized commits.

## Testing
- Add or update automated tests with implementation changes.
- Do not mark work complete solely because code compiles.
- Failed mandatory tests are stop conditions.
- Never use real patient data in local or CI environments.

## Required workflow
PLAN → PRECHECK → IMPLEMENT → TEST → REVIEW → REPORT

Before implementation:
- inspect Git status;
- identify relevant source-of-truth documents;
- identify exact scope and non-scope;
- identify dependencies and risks.

After implementation:
- run relevant tests;
- inspect diff;
- verify no unrelated changes;
- report files changed, tests run/results, DB/API/security impact, assumptions, risks, and next step.

## Stop conditions
STOP if:
- source documents conflict;
- a required business rule is undefined;
- an approved architecture decision must change;
- a destructive DB migration is required without approval;
- a security control would be weakened;
- mandatory tests fail;
- tenant isolation cannot be proven;
- appointment atomicity is violated;
- notification provider calls occur inside a business transaction;
- existing application code would be overwritten;
- production credentials or real patient data would be required;
- unrelated changes become necessary.

## Definition of done
A task is not complete merely because code compiles. It is complete only when implementation, tests, security requirements, relevant contracts, evidence, and traceability are satisfied.
